The hypothesis is false. When a Ctrl-C lands while a tool is running, the next prompt does not send a tool_use without its tool_result, so nothing needed fixing. I committed a regression test that pins this (25160e9f, "Part of #988").

How I tested it: I used yoagent's own mock provider, which panics on an unpaired tool_use the same way a real provider returns a 400. The test copies the REPL's Ctrl-C path: start a prompt, wait until the tool starts, call agent.abort(), return without draining, call finish(), then send a second prompt. I ran it against three tools:

| Tool | History after cancel | Second prompt |
|---|---|---|
| yoyo's real bash tool running sleep 30 | tool_use + toolResult marked as an error, text "Cancelled" | answered |
| a stub that stops when cancelled | tool_use + toolResult marked as an error, text "Cancelled" | answered |
| a stub that ignores the cancel (finishes 300ms later) | tool_use + a normal (non-error) toolResult | answered |

Why it holds: yoagent 0.24.2 waits for each tool to finish and records its result before checking for cancellation, so the pair is complete before the run ends. That also means no Aborted message is ever created on this path: the assistant's stop reason is ToolUse. The way yoyo treats Aborted stop reasons, which prompted the task, never comes into play here.

What the tests check:
• On both turns, every tool_use id has a matching tool_result id, and the second turn's answer actually arrives. The second check matters because a rejected history would otherwise fail silently inside the background task.
• A run with no cancel produces exactly user → assistant (tool call) → tool result → assistant (answer).
• A positive control removes the tool result from the measured history and confirms both checks catch it. Without that, the "paired" result could have been passing for the wrong reason.

Files: a new src/prompt/cancel_mid_tool_tests.rs, plus a two-line test-only mod line in src/prompt.rs. fmt, clippy (-D warnings), build and the full test suite all passed.

On #988: I posted the raw readings and the result. I filed no new issues: the raw readings showed no other defect. I noted two things on #988 without filing them. The cancelled result text is just "Cancelled", and a tool that ignores cancellation keeps running to the end after Ctrl-C. Q1 (is the interrupt message printed?) and Q2 (side effects of the error path) are still open.
